## Why a table is not a message

`/help` printed sixty-one lines into the conversation, where they stayed. An
answer the operator has to scroll past for the rest of the session to reach the
thing they actually asked. That is not what a reference table is for: it is
something you open, look at, and close.

## A panel is data

    {:title \"itonami help\"
     :tabs [{:label \"General\" :lines [...]} …]
     :tab 0 :scroll 0}

A slash handler returns `{:panel …}` and the REPL opens it -- a fourth thing a
handler can return, alongside `:handled`, `:resend` and a promise. Nothing else
about the registry changed, so any command can show instead of say.

## It takes over the block

While a panel is open there is nothing to type into and nothing to send, so a
prompt and a status bar full of keys that do nothing would be two lies at once.
The panel replaces the input area, the cursor is hidden, and the keys mean:

    esc / enter   close
    tab / → ←     next / previous tab (wrapping, so neither end is a dead key)
    ↑ ↓           scroll
    ctrl+d        still leaves -- whatever is on the screen, the way out works

Everything else does nothing. A key that edits a buffer nobody can see is a key
that loses what it typed the moment the panel closes.

## Two things the frame has to do

**Keep its height.** A tab with two lines and a tab with forty produce the same
number of rows, padded. Otherwise switching tabs moves everything under the
panel, and on a terminal that is a redraw the block has to be measured through.

**Say where it is.** `(3-22/29)` in the footer, and the scroll is clamped to
the end in the RENDERER, not only where the key is handled -- so a scroll value
past the end still shows the end rather than an empty frame.

## What `/help` shows

Four tabs: General (what this is, and every key), Commands (the twenty-nine
built-ins), Project (the thirty-two this repository brings, with a line saying
where they came from and that they change with the repository), CLI (the
`itonami …` forms and the exit codes). The Project tab is absent in a
repository that has none, rather than empty.

## Measured

On a pty: `/help` opens with `[General]` marked; tab switches to Commands and
↓ scrolls it, footer reading `(3-22/29)`; esc closes and typing resumes with
the input frame back.

Three controls, each failing by name: a panel that does not take over (seven
checks); a frame that shrinks to its tab's content; a scroll that is not
clamped to the end.
